Kremti Sam-Hum-Mal-Law Snickersnee-katana double wielding...

This is my first ascension and it took a few months since downloading the
game.  Here's how it went.

I was getting bored with Asheron's Call, and needed distraction.  I had
played very old MMO called Island of Kesmai and Drakkar, which are basically
'multiplayer/real-time-rogue' back in the day, and I've also played the old
roguelikes, like the rogue, LARN, moria and such, so I figured tried this a
go...

First problem: Computer problem.  Currently, my computer is a laptop, and
had no numerical keypad.  This game is excruciatingly annoying to play
without.  So I gave up for the time being (I wasn't sure if I wanted to go
buy the keypad for a free game...)

Well, I broke down and I spent 20 bucks after a couple of months :p

Second problem: Newbie problem.  I started to play without much research.  I
tend to study games too much, and seeing this is an open source-code game,
there's a big problem for me to over-analyze too much of this game without
being able to enjoy the 'discovery' part of this game very much.  So, no
spoilers for me in the beginning.  Let's see...fighter classes are always
easier to start with.  Let me try Samurai.  Hey, I got a dog named Hachi.
Cool.  Someone musta heard the story about the Akita...

I move around.  Pick up things.  I have no idea how good they are.  Let me
put that on.  Egad, cursed.  Eeek, the first scroll I read gave me ball and
chain.  Neat, fountain.  Let me drink from that...eeeeek, whole a lotta
snakes.  Dead.

Let me try a wizard.  This 'not being able to identify items' bugs me.
Well, wizards don't start with identify spell I guess.  Anyways, zap zap
zap, pick things up.  I have no idea what they are still.  Hm, I'm getting
hungry and I don't have any food.  Eeek, I'm weak.  I faint.  dead.

I played like this, about once a week, never gotten through more than lvl 5.
A few problems are:

Not aware of prayer and it's primary use in the early game for the hunger
control, I died *A LOT* from the lack of food or just fainting and monsters
having a way with me.  I just couldn't see how this game is enjoyable
without having some sort of ways to restore the hunger other than finding
actual food in the dungeon randomly (I wasn't eating those kobold corpses
either...)

Not being able to ID anything which means I'm going through the dungeon with
*only* using the starting gear or risking cursed item messing me up.  So I
always end up with a backpack full of unidentified neat toys that I have no
idea what they are and it's rather frustrating.

Worse with stores, since I couldn't afford anything when I was on lvl 2 and
3 anyways.  They were just mocking me with all those toys I couldn't touch
:(

I also couldn't tell what those structures like fountains, alters, sinks,
etcs do, so I either tried'em and horribly die, or just pass them by not
knowing what they do.  So here they are, standing in the dungeon as some
sort of fun amusement park ride which I just had to pass by because I have
no idea what to do with them...

I tried all the classes too.  I liked ranger and rogue, since I like
somewhat of finesse characters that are somewhat hybrid.  I particularly
liked ranger for the archery power.

So, back to researching.  www.google.com  Type 'nethack spoiler'.

There we go.

First thing to research is how to combat this hunger problem.  This
obviously leads to the discovery of prayer.  Now I never have to worry about
dying of hunger anymore.

Second thing to research is 'how to identify' those items which also leads
to what to do with those stores on second and third floors.  Use them to
figure out the value of items, find out which one is identify scrolls, and
then so on.  Cool, now I have some sort of plan.  Wait...I can let my pet
steal stuff from the stores?  Cool.  Now all those store clerks who were
mocking me with all the toys will now be cursing about the shoplifters!

Third thing to research is what de heck I am suppose to do with the
fountains, alters, sinks, etcs.  Alters are good for players and B/U/C
status of items.  Very useful.  Other stuff doesn't look too useful to me.
Oh, wait.  I can dip longsword into a fountain and easily get Excalibur if I
am lawful?  Hm, that's useful.  I can also dip unimportant potions into
them, dilute'em into water, and make holy water at the alter, which seems
very handy to me...

A bit more research yields information about weapons and weapon skills.  I
never even knew how to raise the skills.  Cool.  Now I have a plan.

Samurai seems to have a very nice synergy with the skills and starting
equipment.  Starting with katana, he can use it to raise the skill in
longsword.  When it's up to expert, I can double wield it.  If I find a
longsword, I can easily get Excalibur, and switch the katana into the
off-hand weapon.  Also comes with yumi and yas which are very good range
attacks.  Sounds like a good plan to me.

I break my 'lvl 5 barrier' and started to go deeper into the dungeons and be
able to identify stuff a bit.  I even had a Samurai who was doing very well
until he got in trouble with a trap and unicorn in the Mines.  Then, I
finally got a big break...

I found a mine town lighting shop with *2* magic lamps!  W00t!

I bless both of them, and use one of them to get a wish.  The other one is a
light source for now.  I got a +2 SDSM with the wish (which, in retrospect,
